@page "/learn/virtualize-csp"
@layout LearnLayout

<PageTitle>Virtualize under a strict CSP · Field Guide</PageTitle>

<article class="l-article" data-learn>
    <h1 class="l-title">Virtualize under a strict CSP</h1>
    <p class="l-sub">Your Content-Security-Policy bans inline styles. Why do your virtualized lists stop working?</p>
    <p class="l-meta">.NET 11 · Grids &amp; lists · Interactive Server · about 5 minutes</p>
    <p class="l-new">
        <b>What's new in .NET 11:</b> a fix, not an API. <code>Virtualize</code> no longer writes inline styles, so it
        works under a policy without <code>'unsafe-inline'</code>. Your code doesn't change. In .NET 10 the same list
        broke under that policy, silently.
    </p>

    <p>
        A Content-Security-Policy tells the browser what a page may load and apply. A strict one says
        <code>style-src 'self'</code>: styles come from your stylesheets, and nothing else. Every
        <code>style="…"</code> attribute in the markup is refused.
    </p>
    <p>
        We built a small .NET 10 app with that policy: a <code>Virtualize</code> of 1,000 rows, <i>Order #1</i> to
        <i>Order #1000</i>, each 40px tall. This app runs .NET 11, where the bug is fixed, so it can't run the .NET 10
        list itself. Instead, Fig. 1 serves the exact markup that .NET 10 app rendered, under the same policy. Your
        browser refuses it now, just as it did then.
    </p>

    <DemoFigure Number="1" Title="The order list, replayed from .NET 10" Url="demo/virtualize-csp/orders/net10"
                Label=".NET 10 · recorded" Side="before">
        <Caption>
            At first it looks fine. The only hint is the scrollbar thumb: it fills about half the track, as if the
            list held 19 rows, not 1,000. Scroll the list to the end. It stops at <b>Order #19</b>. There's no error
            on screen. Open DevTools → Console to see why: "Applying inline style violates the following Content
            Security Policy directive".
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <h2><small>The problem</small>The spacers are inline styles</h2>
    <p>
        <code>Virtualize</code> only renders the rows near the scroll position. Above and below them it puts a spacer:
        an empty <code>div</code> as tall as the rows it stands for. That gives the box its scroll range. In .NET 10,
        each spacer's height was an inline style:
    </p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">What .NET 10 rendered, and what the browser did with it</span>
        <pre class="snip">&lt;div <span class="err">style="height: 0px; flex-shrink: 0;"</span>&gt;&lt;/div&gt;       <span class="err">refused</span>
&lt;div class="row"&gt;Order #1&lt;/div&gt;
<span class="dim">... Order #2 to #18 ...</span>
&lt;div class="row"&gt;Order #19&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div <span class="err">style="height: 39240px; flex-shrink: 0;"</span>&gt;&lt;/div&gt;   <span class="err">refused: 0px</span></pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        The policy refuses both styles, so the spacers collapse to nothing. The .NET 10 app measured a scroll range
        of 760px, where 1,000 rows of 40px need 40,000px. Order #20 onwards can't be reached. Nothing throws, and the
        page doesn't say why.
    </p>

    <h2><small>The fix</small>Upgrade. The list stays the same</h2>
    <p>There's no code to change. .NET 11's <code>Virtualize</code> renders its spacers differently:</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">The same spacer in .NET 11</span>
        <pre class="snip"><span class="dim">&lt;!-- .NET 10 --&gt;</span>
&lt;div <span class="err">style="height: 39240px; flex-shrink: 0;"</span>&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

<span class="dim">&lt;!-- .NET 11 --&gt;</span>
&lt;div <span class="hl">data-blazor-virtualize-reserved-height="39240"</span>&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        The height is now data. Blazor's script reads it and sets <code>element.style.height</code>. A CSP checks
        styles written into the markup, not ones script sets on an element, so the height stands. Here's a list running
        on .NET 11, under the same policy. It's this app's real order history rather than the test app's numbered
        rows, but it's the same <code>Virtualize</code> with no workaround, and it's longer.
    </p>

    <DemoFigure Number="2" Title="An order list on .NET 11, same policy" Url="demo/virtualize-csp/orders"
                Label=".NET 11 · live">
        <Caption>
            Scroll the list to the end: it reaches the oldest order (the count is above the list), and rows render as you go. The
            dashed line above it stays grey: its own <code>style="…"</code> asks for red, and the policy refuses it.
            So the policy is on, and the list works anyway. The Console logs nothing for the list. (Newest first
            means by the time each order was placed, so order numbers from the same day aren't in sequence.)
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <p>
        Here's the code behind both figures, in the same order. None of it is a change for you to make: the fix is
        the upgrade. The highlighting marks what to look at in each.
    </p>

    <CodeFigure Number="3" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/VirtualizeCsp/OrdersNet10.razor" Feature="virtualize-csp">
        <Caption>
            Fig. 1: the rows and spacers the .NET 10 app rendered, copied as they were, and served with the same
            policy as Fig. 2. Highlighted: the two spacers your browser refuses.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <CodeFigure Number="4" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/VirtualizeCsp/Orders.razor" Feature="virtualize-csp">
        <Caption>
            Fig. 2's page. Highlighted: the policy, <code>style-src 'self'</code> with no <code>'unsafe-inline'</code>.
            This is the test setup, not the fix.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <CodeFigure Number="5" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/VirtualizeCsp/OrderList.razor" Feature="virtualize-csp">
        <Caption>
            The list in Fig. 2. Highlighted: an ordinary <code>&lt;Virtualize&gt;</code>, unchanged from .NET 10. The
            comment above it shows how its spacers render in each version.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <h2><small>The .NET 10 way</small>Weaken the policy, or drop Virtualize</h2>
    <p>
        A nonce doesn't help, because nonces don't cover style attributes. Hashes only do with
        <code>'unsafe-hashes'</code>, and you'd need one for every height a spacer could ever have. That left two
        choices:
    </p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">Two ways round it in .NET 10, and what they cost</span>
        <div class="flow">
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">style-src 'self' <span class="err">'unsafe-inline'</span></pre>
                <span class="why">allow inline styles after all; the .NET 10 app then scrolled to Order #1000</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ injected markup can restyle the page, say to hide a warning, and security reviews flag it</div>
            </div>
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">@@foreach (var order in orders)
{
    &lt;OrderRow Order="order" /&gt;
}</pre>
                <span class="why">don't virtualize</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ every row is on the page, so a long list is slow to load and to scroll</div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <h2><small>.NET 11</small>A strict policy and a virtualized list</h2>
    <p>
        In .NET 11 the spacers carry their height as data, and Blazor's script applies it. The same test app, built on
        .NET 11 with the same policy and the same code, scrolled the whole list and logged no CSP errors. It works in every interactive
        render mode, and for QuickGrid's virtualized rows too. That's what Fig. 2 runs.
    </p>

    <h2><small>Good to know</small></h2>
    <ul class="gk">
        <li><b>DevTools shows <code>style="height: …"</code> on .NET 11 spacers too.</b> That isn't markup. It's the height the script set, which DevTools displays as an attribute. The policy never sees it.</li>
        <li><b>Your own inline styles are refused too,</b> like the dashed line's in Fig. 2. Move them into stylesheets.</li>
        <li><b>A policy belongs to a document.</b> Enhanced navigation patches the next page into the current one, so the next page's header is ignored. That's why this app's Support screen (<code>/support</code>, its ticket desk) sends its policy in a <code>&lt;meta&gt;</code> tag as well as the header.</li>
        <li><b>Lock down scripts as well.</b> This demo only restricts styles. A full policy also bans inline scripts, and Blazor writes its import map inline, so allow that one script by its hash. The Support screen does this.</li>
    </ul>
</article>
